-- CISSP Domain 8 (Software Development Security) expansion: adds 8.1 SDLC models/shift-left/DevSecOps culture, 8.2 dev ecosystem (SCM branching, secrets in CI/CD, SAST/DAST/IAST pipeline integration), 8.3 software security risk (malware taxonomy, buffer overflow, covert channels), 8.4 acquired software (SCA, transitive deps, OSS licensing, vendor vetting), 8.5 secure coding guidelines (XSS/CSRF/SSRF/IDOR, OWASP categories, rate-limit algorithms, ASVS).

insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
-- 8.1 core
('ci-f-1900','cissp','core',8,'8.1',$$What is the Waterfall SDLC model, and what is its main security drawback?$$,$$A strictly sequential model - requirements, design, implementation, verification, maintenance - where each phase must be completed and signed off before the next begins. Its main security drawback is that going back to fix a flaw found late (e.g., in verification) is slow and expensive, since the model wasn't built for iteration.$$),
('ci-f-1901','cissp','core',8,'8.1',$$What is the V-Model, and how does it differ from Waterfall?$$,$$An extension of Waterfall that pairs each development phase with a corresponding testing/validation phase planned in parallel from the start (e.g., requirements <-> acceptance testing, architecture design <-> integration testing). This makes testing - including security testing - a planned deliverable of each phase rather than an afterthought, unlike plain Waterfall.$$),
('ci-f-1902','cissp','core',8,'8.1',$$What distinguishes the Spiral model from other SDLC models?$$,$$The Spiral model wraps iterative development around a formal risk-analysis step performed at the start of every loop/iteration, making it well suited to large, high-risk projects where identifying and addressing risk early, repeatedly, matters more than speed.$$),
('ci-f-1903','cissp','core',8,'8.1',$$How does a Definition of Done (DoD) enforce security in an Agile sprint?$$,$$By adding security acceptance criteria (e.g., "SAST scan shows no high-severity findings," "security story acceptance tests pass") to the team's shared DoD checklist, so a user story cannot be marked complete on functionality alone - it must also clear the agreed security bar.$$),
('ci-f-1904','cissp','core',8,'8.1',$$What is a misuse case (or abuse case), and when is it used?$$,$$A requirements-analysis technique, used during design, that models how a malicious actor could deliberately misuse a feature - complementing normal use cases so security requirements are identified and designed for before any code is written, rather than discovered later as bugs.$$),
('ci-f-1905','cissp','core',8,'8.1',$$What is a security champion, and what does the role NOT replace?$$,$$A developer embedded in a delivery team who advocates for secure practices, triages routine security questions, and liaises with the central AppSec team. It does not replace a dedicated security team - champions handle day-to-day awareness and light triage, while deep expertise (threat modeling review, incident response) still comes from AppSec.$$),
('ci-f-1906','cissp','core',8,'8.1',$$What is the difference between BSIMM and OWASP SAMM?$$,$$BSIMM (Building Security In Maturity Model) is descriptive - it measures what real organizations actually do, built from observed data across many firms, used as a benchmark. SAMM (Software Assurance Maturity Model) is prescriptive - it defines a structured model of what an organization should do to improve, used as a roadmap.$$),
('ci-f-1907','cissp','core',8,'8.1',$$What does "shift-right" security mean, and how does it complement shift-left?$$,$$Extending security practices into production - continuous monitoring, canary/staged releases, and runtime observability that catch issues shift-left testing couldn't predict. Shift-left catches flaws before release; shift-right catches what only shows up under real production conditions.$$),
-- 8.2 core
('ci-f-1908','cissp','core',8,'8.2',$$What is the difference between trunk-based development and GitFlow?$$,$$Trunk-based development uses short-lived branches merged into a single trunk/main branch frequently (often daily), enabling continuous integration and fast feedback. GitFlow uses longer-lived develop, feature, and release branches with more structure but more merge overhead and slower integration back to main.$$),
('ci-f-1909','cissp','core',8,'8.2',$$What do branch protection rules enforce on a repository?$$,$$Platform-enforced requirements - such as passing status checks (build, tests, SAST) and a minimum number of approving reviews - that must be satisfied before a pull request can be merged into a protected branch like main, so the rule can't be bypassed by convention alone.$$),
('ci-f-1910','cissp','core',8,'8.2',$$What is the recommended way to handle secrets used by a CI/CD pipeline?$$,$$Store them in a dedicated secrets manager or vault (e.g., a cloud KMS or HashiCorp Vault) and inject them into the pipeline at runtime with least-privilege scope and rotation - never hardcode them in pipeline configuration files or source code.$$),
('ci-f-1911','cissp','core',8,'8.2',$$Why does secret scanning need to cover git history, not just the latest commit?$$,$$A secret removed from the current version of a file is still retrievable from the repository's commit history unless that history is also scanned (and, if found, purged/rotated) - scanning only the newest diff misses credentials already merged into earlier commits.$$),
('ci-f-1912','cissp','core',8,'8.2',$$What is IAST, and how does it differ from SAST and DAST?$$,$$Interactive Application Security Testing instruments a running application during functional/QA testing, combining SAST's visibility into the code path with DAST's real runtime execution - catching issues with fewer false positives than either technique run alone.$$),
('ci-f-1913','cissp','core',8,'8.2',$$What is a build pipeline (CI/CD) poisoning attack?$$,$$An attack where the adversary compromises the build system itself - a build server, a shared runner, or a malicious plugin/dependency - so that even legitimately-sourced application code gets a malicious payload injected into it during the build, rather than the attacker modifying the source directly.$$),
('ci-f-1914','cissp','core',8,'8.2',$$What does artifact signing and build provenance (as in the SLSA framework) protect against?$$,$$It cryptographically proves which build process produced a given artifact and that the artifact hasn't been altered since - letting downstream consumers verify a binary or container image genuinely came from the expected pipeline rather than being tampered with or substituted afterward.$$),
('ci-f-1915','cissp','core',8,'8.2',$$Why should CI/CD service accounts follow least privilege?$$,$$A pipeline automation account should hold only the specific permissions its job needs (e.g., deploy to one environment) rather than broad admin-level credentials - if the pipeline is compromised, the attacker inherits only that narrow scope instead of full infrastructure control.$$),
('ci-f-1916','cissp','core',8,'8.2',$$What is a pre-commit hook, and what security value does it add?$$,$$A local, developer-side script that runs automatically before a commit is created - performing checks like linting, basic tests, or secret scanning - catching issues before code ever reaches the shared repository, earlier than any CI pipeline stage could.$$),
-- 8.3 core
('ci-f-1917','cissp','core',8,'8.3',$$How does a virus differ from a worm in how it spreads?$$,$$A virus attaches itself to a host file or program and needs that host to be executed - typically via user action, like opening an infected attachment - to spread. A worm spreads autonomously across a network by exploiting a vulnerability, needing neither a host file nor user interaction.$$),
('ci-f-1918','cissp','core',8,'8.3',$$What is "double extortion" ransomware, and why does it defeat backups alone?$$,$$Beyond encrypting a victim's files, double-extortion ransomware first exfiltrates a copy of the data and threatens to publish it if the ransom isn't paid. Restoring from backup recovers availability but does nothing to stop the confidentiality threat of the stolen copy being leaked.$$),
('ci-f-1919','cissp','core',8,'8.3',$$What is a rootkit, and why is it hard to detect?$$,$$Malware that modifies operating-system or kernel-level components to hide its own files, processes, and network connections while maintaining privileged, persistent access - because it subverts the very OS functions normal detection tools rely on to see what's running.$$),
('ci-f-1920','cissp','core',8,'8.3',$$What is a botnet, and what role does C2 infrastructure play?$$,$$A botnet is a network of compromised "zombie" machines an attacker controls remotely, typically to launch DDoS attacks, send spam, or mine cryptocurrency. Command-and-control (C2) infrastructure is how the attacker issues instructions to and receives data from every bot in the network.$$),
('ci-f-1921','cissp','core',8,'8.3',$$What is a logic bomb?$$,$$Malicious code that lies dormant inside a system until a specific trigger condition is met - a date, an event, or the absence of an action (like a disgruntled administrator's account being deactivated) - at which point it executes its damaging payload.$$),
('ci-f-1922','cissp','core',8,'8.3',$$What makes fileless malware difficult for traditional antivirus to catch?$$,$$It operates entirely in memory using legitimate, already-trusted system tools (such as PowerShell or WMI) rather than writing a malicious executable to disk - so there is no file for signature-based antivirus to scan and match against known malware signatures.$$),
('ci-f-1923','cissp','core',8,'8.3',$$What is a buffer overflow, as a software security risk?$$,$$A flaw where a program writes more data into a fixed-size memory buffer than it was allocated to hold, overwriting adjacent memory. If the overwritten memory includes control data like a return address, an attacker can potentially redirect execution to run injected code.$$),
('ci-f-1924','cissp','core',8,'8.3',$$What is a covert channel?$$,$$A communication path that uses a resource or mechanism not intended for information transfer (e.g., subtly varying CPU load, disk usage patterns, or packet timing) to secretly exfiltrate data or signal between processes, bypassing the system's normal, monitored channels.$$),
-- 8.4 core
('ci-f-1925','cissp','core',8,'8.4',$$What is a transitive dependency, and why does it matter for software risk?$$,$$A dependency that your code doesn't directly declare but that one of your direct dependencies relies on internally. Your application still executes that code and inherits any of its vulnerabilities, even though no one on your team ever chose to add it.$$),
('ci-f-1926','cissp','core',8,'8.4',$$What is a dependency confusion attack?$$,$$An attacker publishes a malicious package to a public package registry using the exact name of an organization's internal, private package. If the build system is misconfigured to prefer the public registry (or a higher version number wins), it pulls and executes the attacker's malicious package instead of the intended internal one.$$),
('ci-f-1927','cissp','core',8,'8.4',$$What is typosquatting in the context of package registries?$$,$$Publishing a malicious package under a name that is a common misspelling or close variant of a popular legitimate package (e.g., "reqeusts" instead of "requests"), hoping a developer's typo or misremembered name installs the malicious version instead.$$),
('ci-f-1928','cissp','core',8,'8.4',$$What is the practical difference between copyleft and permissive open-source licenses?$$,$$Copyleft licenses (e.g., GPL) require that derivative or combined works also be distributed under the same license terms - often called "viral" - which can force proprietary code to be open-sourced. Permissive licenses (e.g., MIT, Apache 2.0) impose few restrictions and allow reuse in proprietary products.$$),
('ci-f-1929','cissp','core',8,'8.4',$$What does license compliance scanning check for, separate from vulnerability scanning?$$,$$It inventories the open-source licenses attached to every dependency in use and flags any whose terms (e.g., a copyleft obligation) conflict with the organization's intended distribution or commercial licensing model - a legal/business risk distinct from a security vulnerability.$$),
('ci-f-1930','cissp','core',8,'8.4',$$What should a vendor security assessment review before acquiring third-party software?$$,$$Evidence of the vendor's security posture - such as a SOC 2 report, recent penetration test results, their vulnerability disclosure and patching history, and contractual security/SLA commitments - before the organization takes on the risk of integrating or depending on their product.$$),
('ci-f-1931','cissp','core',8,'8.4',$$Why is running end-of-life (EOL) software a persistent, growing risk?$$,$$Once a vendor stops supporting a product, it no longer receives security patches. Any vulnerability discovered after the EOL date remains permanently unpatched and exploitable for as long as the software stays in use, with the risk only compounding over time.$$),
('ci-f-1932','cissp','core',8,'8.4',$$What problem do dependency pinning and lockfiles solve?$$,$$They lock every dependency (including transitive ones) to an exact, tested version, so a build is reproducible and a new, unreviewed version of a library can't be silently pulled in and deployed just because it was published upstream since the last build.$$),
('ci-f-1933','cissp','core',8,'8.4',$$How can a trusted vendor's own software update become a supply chain attack?$$,$$If an attacker compromises the vendor's build or update-distribution process, every customer who installs the next "legitimate" signed update receives the attacker's malicious code too - the update channel itself becomes the attack vector, bypassing the trust customers place in the vendor.$$),
-- 8.5 core
('ci-f-1934','cissp','core',8,'8.5',$$What are the three main types of XSS, and what is the primary defense?$$,$$Reflected XSS (payload bounces off a single request/response), stored XSS (payload is saved server-side and served to later victims), and DOM-based XSS (payload executes purely through client-side script manipulation). The primary defense is contextual output encoding of any user-controlled data, reinforced by a Content Security Policy.$$),
('ci-f-1935','cissp','core',8,'8.5',$$How does CSRF work, and what stops it?$$,$$CSRF tricks an already-authenticated victim's browser into submitting a forged request (e.g., via a malicious link or auto-submitting form) to a site the victim is logged into, performing an action the victim never intended. It's defended against with unpredictable anti-CSRF tokens and the SameSite cookie attribute, which stops cookies from being sent on cross-site requests.$$),
('ci-f-1936','cissp','core',8,'8.5',$$What is SSRF, and why is it dangerous even against a well-firewalled network?$$,$$Server-Side Request Forgery tricks a server into making an unintended request on the attacker's behalf - often to internal-only systems or cloud metadata endpoints the attacker could never reach directly. Because the request originates from the trusted server itself, it can bypass network-perimeter firewall rules meant to keep outsiders out.$$),
('ci-f-1937','cissp','core',8,'8.5',$$What is Broken Access Control, and where does it rank in the OWASP Top 10 (2021)?$$,$$The #1-ranked OWASP Top 10 (2021) category, covering any failure that lets a user act outside their intended permissions - including missing function-level checks and object-level authorization failures like IDOR.$$),
('ci-f-1938','cissp','core',8,'8.5',$$What is an IDOR (Insecure Direct Object Reference) vulnerability?$$,$$A flaw where an application exposes a direct, guessable reference to an internal object - such as a sequential record ID in a URL or API call - without verifying that the requesting user is actually authorized to access that specific object, letting an attacker view or modify others' data by changing the ID.$$),
('ci-f-1939','cissp','core',8,'8.5',$$What layered defenses do secure coding guidelines recommend against buffer overflow exploitation?$$,$$Prefer memory-safe languages where feasible; use bounds-checked/safe string and memory functions instead of unsafe legacy equivalents; and rely on OS-level protections like ASLR (address space layout randomization) and DEP/NX (non-executable memory) as defense-in-depth even if a bug slips through.$$),
('ci-f-1940','cissp','core',8,'8.5',$$What is the difference between the token bucket and leaky bucket rate-limiting algorithms?$$,$$Token bucket allows controlled bursts of traffic up to the bucket's capacity as long as tokens are available, then throttles once they're spent. Leaky bucket smooths traffic to a strictly constant output rate regardless of how bursty the input is, discarding what exceeds that rate.$$),
('ci-f-1941','cissp','core',8,'8.5',$$What is a security misconfiguration, as an OWASP Top 10 category?$$,$$Shipping or deploying software with unsafe defaults - default/unchanged credentials, unnecessary services or features left enabled, or overly verbose error messages that leak stack traces and internal system details to end users.$$),
('ci-f-1942','cissp','core',8,'8.5',$$What is OWASP ASVS, and what problem does it solve?$$,$$The Application Security Verification Standard - a framework of specific, testable security requirements organized into increasing verification levels, used to define and agree on "how secure is secure enough" for a given application rather than leaving that judgment informal or inconsistent.$$),
-- acronyms 8.1
('ci-a-610','cissp','acronym',8,'8.1',$$CI/CD$$,$$Continuous Integration / Continuous Delivery (or Deployment) - the automated pipeline practice of frequently integrating, testing, and releasing code changes; the backbone DevSecOps embeds security checks into.$$),
('ci-a-611','cissp','acronym',8,'8.1',$$BSIMM$$,$$Building Security In Maturity Model - a descriptive, observation-based benchmark of what real organizations actually do for software security, used to compare against industry practice.$$),
('ci-a-612','cissp','acronym',8,'8.1',$$SAMM$$,$$(OWASP) Software Assurance Maturity Model - a prescriptive framework defining a maturity roadmap an organization can follow to improve its software security practices.$$),
-- acronyms 8.2
('ci-a-613','cissp','acronym',8,'8.2',$$SAST$$,$$Static Application Security Testing - analyzes source or binary code without executing it, early and across the whole codebase.$$),
('ci-a-614','cissp','acronym',8,'8.2',$$DAST$$,$$Dynamic Application Security Testing - tests a running application from the outside, exercising real runtime behavior.$$),
('ci-a-615','cissp','acronym',8,'8.2',$$IAST$$,$$Interactive Application Security Testing - instruments a running app during testing, combining code-level visibility with runtime accuracy.$$),
-- acronyms 8.3
('ci-a-616','cissp','acronym',8,'8.3',$$RAT$$,$$Remote Access Trojan - malware disguised as legitimate software that, once installed, gives an attacker covert remote control over the victim's machine.$$),
('ci-a-617','cissp','acronym',8,'8.3',$$C2$$,$$Command and Control - the infrastructure an attacker uses to issue instructions to and receive data from compromised/bot-infected machines.$$),
('ci-a-618','cissp','acronym',8,'8.3',$$TOCTOU$$,$$Time-Of-Check to Time-Of-Use - a race condition where a resource's state is validated, then changed by another process before it's actually used, invalidating the earlier check.$$),
-- acronyms 8.4
('ci-a-619','cissp','acronym',8,'8.4',$$SCA$$,$$Software Composition Analysis - scans an application's dependencies to identify known-vulnerable or improperly licensed open-source/third-party components.$$),
('ci-a-620','cissp','acronym',8,'8.4',$$COTS$$,$$Commercial Off-The-Shelf (software) - a pre-built product purchased from a vendor rather than developed in-house, requiring its own vendor security assessment before adoption.$$),
('ci-a-621','cissp','acronym',8,'8.4',$$EOL$$,$$End Of Life - the point at which a vendor stops supporting a product, after which it receives no further security patches.$$),
('ci-a-622','cissp','acronym',8,'8.4',$$GPL$$,$$GNU General Public License - a copyleft open-source license requiring derivative/combined works to also be released under GPL terms.$$),
-- acronyms 8.5
('ci-a-623','cissp','acronym',8,'8.5',$$OWASP$$,$$Open Web Application Security Project - the nonprofit foundation behind widely-used resources like the OWASP Top 10 and ASVS for web application security.$$),
('ci-a-624','cissp','acronym',8,'8.5',$$XSS$$,$$Cross-Site Scripting - injecting malicious script into content that renders in another user's browser session.$$),
('ci-a-625','cissp','acronym',8,'8.5',$$CSRF$$,$$Cross-Site Request Forgery - forcing an authenticated victim's browser to unknowingly submit a forged request to a trusted site.$$),
('ci-a-626','cissp','acronym',8,'8.5',$$SSRF$$,$$Server-Side Request Forgery - tricking a server into making an unintended request on the attacker's behalf, often against internal-only resources.$$),
-- feynman 8.1
('ci-y-390','cissp','feynman',8,'8.1',$$Explain why a security flaw caught during Waterfall's design phase is so much cheaper to fix than the same flaw caught after release, and why Agile/DevSecOps structurally reduce that gap.$$,$$Model answer: Cost to fix a defect roughly multiplies at each stage it survives past - a design-phase security flaw might mean rewriting a diagram or requirement, essentially free. The same flaw not caught until coding means rewriting code that colleagues have already built on top of. Caught in a Waterfall project's final verification phase, after months of implementation downstream, it means re-opening design, re-implementing, and re-testing everything that depended on it - and Waterfall's phase-gate structure makes that especially painful because work was never meant to flow backward. If it survives release entirely, fixing it means an emergency patch, regression testing under time pressure, and possibly a live breach with real damage. Agile and DevSecOps structurally shrink this gap by folding security checks (threat modeling, SAST, security acceptance criteria) into every short iteration or every commit, so the same class of flaw is far more likely to be caught within days of being introduced rather than months later - there's simply less already built on top of it, and less to unwind.$$),
('ci-y-391','cissp','feynman',8,'8.1',$$Explain what a security champion actually does day to day, and why the role doesn't substitute for a dedicated AppSec team.$$,$$Model answer: A security champion is a regular developer on a delivery team - not a security specialist by trade - who takes on an extra, recognized role: keeping security visible in day-to-day planning (flagging when a story touches something sensitive), doing a first pass on routine questions ("is it okay to store this in local storage?"), and acting as the go-to contact between their team and the central AppSec group. What the role does not do is replace that AppSec group's depth: a champion isn't expected to run a full threat model on a complex new system, lead incident response, or make the final call on a genuinely novel risk - those still need people whose full-time job is security expertise. The value of the model is coverage and speed: instead of every small security question queuing for a scarce specialist, most get triaged instantly by someone embedded in the team, and only the harder cases escalate.$$),
('ci-y-392','cissp','feynman',8,'8.1',$$Explain the difference between BSIMM and SAMM and why an organization might reasonably use both together.$$,$$Model answer: BSIMM and SAMM answer different questions. BSIMM is descriptive - it's built by observing dozens of real organizations' actual software security programs and cataloging what they do, so using it tells you "here is where firms like us typically are, and what the more mature ones do that we don't." SAMM is prescriptive - it lays out a structured model of practices and maturity levels independent of what anyone else is doing, so using it tells you "here is a coherent path to improve, step by step." An organization might use both because they serve complementary purposes: SAMM gives a roadmap to follow, while BSIMM gives a reality check - benchmarking against actual peer behavior keeps the SAMM roadmap grounded and helps justify investment ("our peers already do this") rather than chasing an abstract ideal no one has actually achieved.$$),
-- feynman 8.2
('ci-y-393','cissp','feynman',8,'8.2',$$Explain why a compromised CI/CD pipeline can be more dangerous than a compromised application, using a concrete build-poisoning example.$$,$$Model answer: An application-level compromise, however bad, is usually scoped to that one app and the data it touches. A compromised CI/CD pipeline is scoped to everything the pipeline builds and ships - which is often every application the organization produces. Concretely: if an attacker plants malicious code in a shared build-runner image or a compromised build plugin, every project that runs through that pipeline gets the malicious payload silently injected during compilation, even though every individual developer's source code stayed completely clean. The resulting artifacts are signed and shipped as if legitimate, because from the pipeline's own perspective nothing looks wrong - it's exactly what happened in real-world build-system compromises. That's why pipeline infrastructure needs to be treated as security-critical in its own right, not just as neutral plumbing between trusted source code and a trusted deployment.$$),
('ci-y-394','cissp','feynman',8,'8.2',$$Explain why hardcoding a secret in source code is dangerous even when the repository is private, walking through how it can still leak.$$,$$Model answer: "Private" describes who can currently view the repository, not how permanently safe a secret embedded in it is. The credential still sits in plaintext in every commit that touched it, which means it's copied into every developer's local clone, every CI runner that checks the repo out, and every backup of the repository - any one of which can leak it regardless of the platform-level visibility setting. Access to the "private" repo can also widen over time: a contractor gets added, an integration tool is granted read access, or the repo is later made public or forked without anyone remembering the old secret is still sitting in history. And even without any of that, if the credential is ever accidentally logged, pasted into a support ticket, or exposed by a misconfigured CI log, its plaintext form in source control makes tracing and revoking it far harder than if it had lived in a secrets vault that could just be rotated centrally in one place.$$),
('ci-y-395','cissp','feynman',8,'8.2',$$Explain the practical difference between SAST, DAST, and IAST and where each best fits in a CI/CD pipeline.$$,$$Model answer: SAST reads the source or binary code without running it - fast, can scan the whole codebase early (even before a build exists), and can point to an exact vulnerable line, but it doesn't know how the code actually behaves at runtime, so it tends to produce false positives on things that are safe in context. DAST runs against a live, deployed application from the outside, black-box style - it confirms a vulnerability is genuinely exploitable in the real running system, but it can only test what it can reach, needs a working environment to test against, and can't point to a specific line of code. IAST sits in between: an agent instrumented inside the running application (during QA/functional testing) sees both the code path being executed and the real runtime data flowing through it, so it can confirm exploitability like DAST while pointing to the exact source location like SAST - at the cost of needing that instrumentation set up. In a pipeline, SAST runs earliest (on every commit), DAST runs later against a staging deployment, and IAST fits naturally during functional/QA test execution in between.$$),
-- feynman 8.3
('ci-y-396','cissp','feynman',8,'8.3',$$Explain how ransomware's double-extortion tactic defeats a victim's backup-based recovery strategy.$$,$$Model answer: A pure encryption-based ransomware attack has one lever against the victim: "pay us, or you can't get your files back." A solid, tested backup completely defeats that lever - restore from backup, wipe the infected systems, and the ransom demand has no teeth. Double extortion adds a second, independent lever that backups do nothing about: before ever triggering encryption, the attacker quietly exfiltrates a copy of the victim's sensitive data. Now the threat isn't just "your files are locked," it's "we already have a copy, and if you don't pay, we'll publish it publicly or sell it." Restoring from backup fixes the availability problem completely, but it can't un-exfiltrate data that already left the network - the confidentiality threat exists independent of whether the victim ever needed the ransom to get their files back, which is exactly why double extortion pressures even organizations with excellent backup discipline.$$),
('ci-y-397','cissp','feynman',8,'8.3',$$Explain how fileless malware evades traditional signature-based antivirus, using PowerShell as a concrete example.$$,$$Model answer: Traditional antivirus largely works by matching files on disk against a database of known-malicious signatures - a hash or byte pattern of a malicious executable. Fileless malware sidesteps that entirely by never writing its own executable to disk. Instead, it abuses tools that are already trusted and already present on the system - for example, a phishing document's macro invokes PowerShell (a legitimate, signed Windows administration tool) and feeds it a malicious script that runs entirely in memory, using PowerShell's own legitimate process to download further payloads, move laterally, or exfiltrate data. From the antivirus's point of view, there's no new suspicious file to hash and check - PowerShell.exe is supposed to be there and is normally benign, so the malicious activity has to be caught by watching what PowerShell is actually doing (behavioral/EDR-style detection) rather than what file it is.$$),
('ci-y-398','cissp','feynman',8,'8.3',$$Explain, with a worked example, how a TOCTOU race condition can be exploited even though each individual check and action looks correct in isolation.$$,$$Model answer: Imagine a program that checks a bank balance is sufficient before authorizing a withdrawal, then performs the withdrawal as a separate later step - "time of check" and "time of use" are two distinct moments, not one atomic operation. If an attacker can trigger a second, concurrent withdrawal request in the gap between the check and the actual debit, both requests can pass the balance check (since neither has been deducted yet) and both withdrawals can succeed, draining more money than the account ever held - even though each individual request, examined on its own, correctly checked the balance before acting. The bug isn't in the logic of either request; it's in the assumption that nothing else could change the resource's state between the check and the use. The fix is to make the check-and-use sequence atomic - for example, using a database transaction with proper locking - so no other process can slip in during that window.$$),
-- feynman 8.4
('ci-y-399','cissp','feynman',8,'8.4',$$Explain why a transitive dependency can introduce a vulnerability into your application even though no one on your team chose to use that library directly.$$,$$Model answer: When your code imports a library, you're not just trusting that library's own code - you're trusting everything that library itself imports, and everything those imports import, all the way down. None of those deeper dependencies were things you evaluated, approved, or even necessarily know exist; they were pulled in automatically as part of satisfying your one direct dependency's own requirements. If a vulnerability is later discovered three layers down that chain, your application still executes that vulnerable code at runtime just as surely as if you'd imported it yourself - the attack surface doesn't care that you never directly typed its name. This is exactly why a full dependency tree (not just your direct imports) has to be scanned by an SCA tool and captured in an SBOM: the risk you're actually exposed to includes every transitive link in the chain, whether you knew about it or not.$$),
('ci-y-400','cissp','feynman',8,'8.4',$$Explain the practical business risk of unknowingly shipping GPL-licensed code inside a proprietary commercial product.$$,$$Model answer: GPL is a copyleft license, meaning that if you incorporate GPL-licensed code into your own software and then distribute that software, the license's terms generally require you to release your combined work under the same GPL terms too - including, in many interpretations, making your own proprietary source code available. If a developer pulls in a convenient GPL-licensed library without anyone reviewing the license, a company can end up unknowingly obligated to open-source software it intended to keep proprietary and sell as a closed product, or find itself in violation of the license (and exposed to a legal claim) the moment it ships. The fix isn't avoiding open source - it's license compliance scanning as a routine part of dependency review, so a copyleft obligation is caught and a decision is made deliberately, before a product ships, rather than discovered after the fact by a lawyer or a competitor.$$),
('ci-y-401','cissp','feynman',8,'8.4',$$Explain how a dependency confusion attack works and why simply giving an internal package a unique name doesn't fully fix the problem.$$,$$Model answer: Many organizations use an internal package registry for private, proprietary packages alongside a public registry (like npm or PyPI) for open-source ones, and configure their build tooling to check both. If the build is set up to prefer whichever registry has the higher version number, or simply checks the public registry first, an attacker who publishes a public package with the exact same name as the internal one - and a higher version number - can get their malicious package pulled and executed instead of the real internal one, with no visible change to any file in the company's own source control. Giving the internal package a unique name (like a company-specific prefix) helps, but doesn't fully fix the underlying problem: it only works if every current and future internal package consistently follows that convention, and it does nothing to stop the build tooling itself from being misconfigured to prefer external sources. The complete fix is scoping/pinning the build to only ever pull that package name from the internal registry, so the public registry is never even consulted for it.$$),
-- feynman 8.5
('ci-y-402','cissp','feynman',8,'8.5',$$Explain how a CSRF attack works step by step, and why the SameSite cookie attribute blocks it.$$,$$Model answer: Say a victim is logged into their bank's website, so their browser is holding a valid session cookie for it. The attacker gets the victim to visit a malicious page - maybe via a phishing link - that silently auto-submits a form (or loads an image tag pointing at a URL) targeting the bank's "transfer funds" endpoint, with the attacker's account as the destination. Because the request goes to the bank's own domain, the victim's browser automatically attaches the valid session cookie, and if the bank's server has no other check, it processes the transfer as if the victim had submitted it themselves - the victim never saw or approved the actual request. A SameSite cookie attribute (set to Strict or Lax) blocks this by telling the browser not to send that cookie at all on a request that originates from a different site than the one the cookie belongs to - so when the malicious page's form submits to the bank, the browser withholds the session cookie, the bank's server sees an unauthenticated request, and the forged transfer fails.$$),
('ci-y-403','cissp','feynman',8,'8.5',$$Explain, with an example, how SSRF lets an attacker pivot from a public-facing web application to attack internal-only infrastructure it could never reach directly.$$,$$Model answer: Picture a web app that lets a user submit a URL for the app to "fetch and preview" - say, generating a thumbnail from a link the user pastes in. The app's server, sitting inside the company's internal network, dutifully fetches whatever URL it's given. Instead of a normal external URL, the attacker submits something like the cloud provider's internal metadata endpoint (a well-known internal-only address that returns instance credentials) or an internal admin panel's address that isn't reachable from the public internet at all. Because the fetch is performed by the trusted server itself - which does sit inside that internal network and does have access - the request succeeds and returns internal data straight back to the attacker through the app's own response, something the attacker could never have reached by connecting directly from outside. The server, in effect, becomes the attacker's proxy into the internal network, which is exactly why SSRF is so dangerous even behind a well-configured perimeter firewall: the firewall was never designed to distrust requests that legitimately originate from inside its own boundary.$$),
('ci-y-404','cissp','feynman',8,'8.5',$$Explain why the token bucket algorithm allows legitimate bursts of traffic while still capping average abuse, contrasting it with strict leaky-bucket throttling.$$,$$Model answer: A token bucket starts full of tokens (say, capacity 100) and refills at a steady rate (say, 10 tokens/second); each request consumes one token, and a request is only allowed if a token is available. Because tokens accumulate while idle, a client that hasn't made requests in a while can suddenly fire off a burst of up to 100 requests all at once and still be allowed, as long as tokens are available - useful for legitimate bursty traffic like a user quickly loading several page resources. Once the bucket empties, though, the client is throttled back down to the steady refill rate. A leaky bucket, in contrast, is only about smoothing: requests come in and queue up, but they leak out (are processed) at a strictly constant rate no matter how bursty the input was - there's no reservoir of saved-up allowance to spend, so even a client that had gone quiet for a while gets exactly the same fixed rate as a client that had been sending requests constantly. Token bucket is more forgiving of natural burstiness; leaky bucket is stricter and prioritizes a perfectly even output rate, which is better suited when downstream systems truly can't handle any spike at all.$$);

insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
-- 8.1 SDLC and shift-left security
('ci-q-1900','cissp',8,'8.1','mcq',1,$q$A Scrum team's Definition of Done requires that a user story cannot be marked complete unless an automated static analysis scan shows zero high-severity findings, in addition to passing its functional acceptance tests. What does adding this security criterion to the Definition of Done accomplish?$q$,$q$["It makes passing a baseline security check as mandatory for completion as passing functional tests, rather than optional or after-the-fact", "It replaces the need for any dedicated security review before release", "It only applies to stories explicitly labeled as security stories", "It guarantees the code contains no vulnerabilities of any kind"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A Definition of Done is a non-negotiable completion checklist. Folding a security acceptance criterion into it means a story simply cannot be called finished on functionality alone - it structurally cannot skip the security bar, though it doesn't replace deeper review or guarantee perfection.$q$),
('ci-q-1901','cissp',8,'8.1','mcq',2,$q$A project team pairs every development phase with a corresponding test phase planned from the outset - for example, system architecture design is paired with a planned integration-test phase before any code is written. Compared to a plain sequential (Waterfall) approach, what security benefit does this specific pairing provide?$q$,$q$["Security and validation testing becomes a planned deliverable of each phase rather than something added only near the end", "It eliminates the need for a formal requirements phase entirely", "It allows work to proceed in the same order as Agile sprints", "It guarantees the project will require no risk analysis"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$This describes the V-Model, which pairs each development phase with a corresponding, planned testing/validation phase from the start - so testing (including security testing) is baked in as a deliverable of each phase, not bolted on only at the end as in plain Waterfall.$q$),
('ci-q-1902','cissp',8,'8.1','mcq',2,$q$A large, high-uncertainty government software project requires a formal risk-analysis step to be repeated at the start of every development loop, before that loop's objectives and constraints are finalized. Which SDLC model does this describe?$q$,$q$["Spiral", "Waterfall", "V-Model", "Agile Scrum"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The Spiral model wraps iterative development around a formal, repeated risk-analysis step performed at the start of every loop, making it especially suited to large, high-risk projects where repeatedly identifying and addressing risk matters more than speed.$q$),
('ci-q-1903','cissp',8,'8.1','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO statements that correctly describe DevSecOps as a security-in-SDLC approach.$q$,$q$["It automates security checks (such as SAST and dependency scanning) so they run continuously throughout the CI/CD pipeline rather than only at a single release gate", "It treats security as a shared responsibility across development, operations, and security teams rather than a separate team's sole concern", "It requires abandoning Agile sprint structure in favor of Waterfall phase gates", "It eliminates the need for any manual security review once automation is in place"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0, 1]$q$::jsonb,$q$DevSecOps automates security checks continuously across the pipeline and makes security a shared, cultural responsibility rather than one team's job. It builds on Agile/iterative delivery rather than reverting to Waterfall, and automation supplements rather than eliminates manual review for complex or novel risks.$q$),
('ci-q-1904','cissp',8,'8.1','mcq',1,$q$Before any code is written, a design team documents how an attacker might deliberately abuse a "password reset" feature - for example, by requesting resets for accounts they don't own to enumerate valid usernames - alongside the normal use case for a legitimate user resetting a forgotten password. What technique is being used?$q$,$q$["A misuse case (abuse case)", "A tabletop exercise", "A penetration test", "A code review checklist"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A misuse case (or abuse case) models how a feature could be deliberately misused, complementing the normal use case during design so security requirements are identified and designed for before any code is written.$q$),
('ci-q-1905','cissp',8,'8.1','mcq',2,$q$An organization wants to know how its software security practices compare to what similar-sized companies in its industry are actually doing in practice, using data gathered by observing many real organizations rather than a theoretical roadmap. Which model is best suited to this specific purpose?$q$,$q$["BSIMM", "SAMM", "OWASP ASVS", "ISO 22301"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$BSIMM (Building Security In Maturity Model) is descriptive - built from observed data across many real organizations - making it the right tool for benchmarking against actual peer practice, unlike SAMM's prescriptive roadmap or ASVS's verification requirements.$q$),
('ci-q-1906','cissp',8,'8.1','mcq',1,$q$A developer embedded on a delivery team fields routine day-to-day security questions from teammates and escalates anything complex to the central application security group, without personally being expected to run a full threat-modeling session or lead incident response. What role is this?$q$,$q$["Security champion", "Chief Information Security Officer", "Penetration tester", "Data protection officer"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A security champion is a developer who advocates for secure practices and triages routine questions within their own team, escalating deeper expertise needs (like formal threat modeling or incident response) to the dedicated AppSec team rather than replacing it.$q$),
('ci-q-1907','cissp',8,'8.1','mcq',2,$q$After release, a team relies on canary deployments and continuous production monitoring to catch a class of failure that only manifests under genuine real-world load and configuration, something no pre-release test environment fully reproduced. What security practice does this describe?$q$,$q$["Shift-right security", "Shift-left security", "A misuse case", "A Definition of Done gate"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Shift-right extends security practices into production - staged/canary releases and runtime observability - catching issues that shift-left testing before release couldn't predict, complementing rather than replacing it.$q$),
('ci-q-1908','cissp',8,'8.1','mcq',2,$q$A vendor proposes building a customer-facing prototype through iterative, rapid, workshop-driven development with heavy end-user involvement and minimal upfront formal documentation, aiming to converge on requirements through repeated demos rather than a detailed specification. Which SDLC model does this best describe, and what is its main security tradeoff?$q$,$q$["Rapid Application Development (RAD) - speed and stakeholder feedback come at the cost of less formal, upfront security requirements analysis and documentation", "Waterfall - phases only ever proceed in strict linear order", "Spiral - risk analysis is repeated at the start of every loop", "V-Model - every phase is paired with a planned test phase"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$RAD prioritizes speed and iterative prototyping with heavy user involvement over comprehensive upfront documentation, which means security requirements analysis - normally done carefully in a formal requirements phase - risks being rushed or informally handled.$q$),
('ci-q-1909','cissp',8,'8.1','ordering',2,$q$Order the following generic SDLC phases from first to last.$q$,$q$["Requirements gathering and analysis", "Design", "Implementation (coding)", "Testing/verification", "Deployment/release", "Maintenance"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5]$q$::jsonb,$q$The classic SDLC flows from gathering and analyzing requirements, through design, into implementation (coding), then testing/verification, deployment/release, and finally ongoing maintenance - the backbone every SDLC model (Waterfall, V-Model, Spiral, Agile) adapts or iterates over in its own way.$q$),
('ci-q-1910','cissp',8,'8.1','matching',3,$q$Match each SDLC model to the characteristic that most specifically defines it.$q$,$q${"left": ["Waterfall", "Spiral", "V-Model", "Agile (Scrum)"], "right": ["Iterative loops, each beginning with a formal risk-analysis step", "Short, fixed-length iterations (sprints) with a re-prioritized backlog at the start of each", "Sequential phases with formal sign-off gates between them, with no built-in path back to an earlier phase", "Every development phase is paired with a corresponding, planned test phase"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2, 0, 3, 1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Waterfall proceeds through strictly sequential, gated phases; Spiral repeats a formal risk-analysis step at the start of every loop; V-Model pairs every development phase with a planned test phase; Agile/Scrum works in short, fixed iterations with a re-prioritized backlog each time.$q$),
-- 8.2 development ecosystem security
('ci-q-1911','cissp',8,'8.2','mcq',1,$q$A developer opens a pull request against the main branch, but the platform automatically blocks merging because the required static analysis check has not yet reported a passing result, even though a teammate already approved the code changes. What is enforcing this block?$q$,$q$["A branch protection rule requiring specific status checks to pass before merge", "A secrets manager", "An IAST agent", "A software bill of materials"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Branch protection rules are platform-enforced requirements - such as passing status checks and a minimum number of approvals - that must be satisfied before a merge into a protected branch can proceed, and they can't be bypassed just because one requirement (review) is already met.$q$),
('ci-q-1912','cissp',8,'8.2','mcq',2,$q$A hardcoded API key is discovered in a file that was committed six months ago and has since been removed from the current version of the codebase. What is the correct remediation, and why?$q$,$q$["Rotate (revoke and reissue) the credential, because it remains permanently retrievable from the repository's commit history even though the current file no longer contains it", "No action is needed, since the file no longer contains the key in its latest version", "Delete the entire repository, since there is no other way to remove a secret from git history", "Rename the file that used to contain the key"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A secret committed to version control stays retrievable from history indefinitely unless that history is scrubbed - and even then, anyone who already cloned it may still have a copy. The only reliable fix is rotating the credential itself, not merely removing it from the current file.$q$),
('ci-q-1913','cissp',8,'8.2','mcq',2,$q$Instead of issuing a database password that stays valid indefinitely, a secrets vault generates a unique credential for each pipeline run that automatically expires after one hour. What security benefit does this dynamic, short-lived approach provide over a static, long-lived credential?$q$,$q$["It shrinks the window during which a leaked credential remains usable by an attacker", "It removes the need for the vault to authenticate the pipeline at all", "It guarantees the pipeline can never be compromised", "It eliminates the need for least-privilege scoping on the credential"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Dynamic, short-lived credentials limit the damage of a leak: even if a token is exposed, it becomes useless once it expires, unlike a static credential that remains exploitable indefinitely until someone notices and manually rotates it.$q$),
('ci-q-1914','cissp',8,'8.2','matching',2,$q$Match each application security testing technique to the description that best fits it.$q$,$q${"left": ["SAST", "DAST", "IAST", "SCA"], "right": ["Uses an agent inside a running application during functional testing to combine code-level and runtime visibility", "Scans an application's open-source and third-party dependencies for known vulnerabilities", "Analyzes source or binary code without executing it, able to scan the entire codebase early", "Tests a running application from the outside, black-box style, confirming real runtime exploitability"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2, 3, 0, 1]$q$::jsonb,$q$SAST analyzes code statically without execution; DAST tests a running application from the outside; IAST instruments a running app during testing to combine both perspectives; SCA scans dependencies specifically for known-vulnerable or improperly licensed components.$q$),
('ci-q-1915','cissp',8,'8.2','mcq',1,$q$A team switches from long-lived feature branches merged monthly to short-lived branches merged into a single trunk multiple times per day. What is the primary benefit of this change from a security-relevant integration standpoint?$q$,$q$["Frequent small merges reduce the size and risk of each individual merge conflict and let automated security checks run against nearly current code continuously", "It removes the need for any code review before merging", "It guarantees zero merge conflicts will ever occur", "It disables the need for branch protection rules"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Trunk-based development's short-lived branches and frequent integration keep each merge small and keep security tooling (SAST, tests) running against code that's always close to current - the opposite of a single, large, risky monthly merge.$q$),
('ci-q-1916','cissp',8,'8.2','mcq',2,$q$A team cuts a dedicated release branch from its develop branch, applies only bug fixes and final security testing to it, and merges it into main once stable, while ongoing feature work continues separately on develop. Which branching model does this describe?$q$,$q$["GitFlow", "Trunk-based development", "Continuous deployment without branching", "Feature-flag-only development"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$This structured use of long-lived develop, feature, and dedicated release branches - with the release branch used to stabilize and harden before merging to main - is the defining pattern of GitFlow, distinct from trunk-based development's frequent single-branch integration.$q$),
('ci-q-1917','cissp',8,'8.2','mcq',3,$q$An attacker who has gained the ability to submit a pull request modifies not the application's source code, but the CI pipeline configuration file itself, adding a step that exfiltrates environment secrets during the build. Why does this specific target matter?$q$,$q$["Pipeline-as-code configuration executes with the pipeline's own privileges and access to secrets, so it needs the same review rigor as application source code, not less", "Pipeline configuration files cannot be modified by a pull request under any circumstances", "This attack is impossible unless the attacker also has direct production server access", "CI pipeline configuration is never able to access secrets under any configuration"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A pipeline definition file is itself executable configuration - a modification to it runs with the pipeline's own privileges and secret access, exactly like build poisoning. Treating pipeline-as-code as lower-scrutiny than application code leaves this exact attack path open.$q$),
('ci-q-1918','cissp',8,'8.2','mcq',2,$q$A consumer downloading a container image can cryptographically verify exactly which build pipeline produced it and confirm the artifact hasn't been altered since that build completed. What concept does this verification rely on?$q$,$q$["Build provenance (as formalized by frameworks like SLSA)", "A trunk-based branching strategy", "A misuse case", "Rate limiting"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Build provenance cryptographically proves which pipeline produced an artifact and that it hasn't been tampered with since - letting a downstream consumer verify authenticity and integrity rather than trusting the artifact's origin blindly.$q$),
('ci-q-1919','cissp',8,'8.2','mcq',1,$q$A repository policy requires that the person who wrote a change may never be the same person who approves its merge. What security principle does this specifically enforce?$q$,$q$["Separation of duties (the \"four-eyes\" principle) applied to code review", "Least privilege for service accounts", "Rate limiting", "Data remanence"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Requiring a different, independent reviewer for every change is a direct application of separation of duties (the "four-eyes" principle) to the code review process, reducing the chance that a single compromised or careless individual can introduce unreviewed changes.$q$),
('ci-q-1920','cissp',8,'8.2','ordering',2,$q$Order the following stages of a typical secure CI/CD pipeline from earliest to latest.$q$,$q$["Developer commits code and a pre-commit hook runs basic checks", "Static analysis (SAST) runs against the new commit", "The application is built and packaged into an artifact", "The artifact is cryptographically signed", "Dynamic analysis (DAST) runs against a deployed staging environment", "The signed artifact is deployed to production"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5]$q$::jsonb,$q$A shift-left pipeline runs cheapest, earliest checks first: a local pre-commit hook, then SAST on the new commit, then the build produces and signs an artifact, then DAST exercises it once deployed to staging, and only after all of that does the signed, tested artifact reach production.$q$),
('ci-q-1921','cissp',8,'8.2','mcq',2,$q$Beyond scanning an application's source code and direct dependencies, a security team also scans every container image stored in the internal image registry for known-vulnerable OS packages before any image can be pulled for deployment. What gap does this additional scanning close?$q$,$q$["Vulnerabilities introduced by the base OS image and packages baked into the container layer, which source-code-level scanning alone would never see", "The need for any SAST scanning of the application's own source code", "The need for branch protection rules on the source repository", "The need for a secrets vault"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Source-level scanning only sees the application's own code and its declared dependencies; it has no visibility into the base OS image and system packages baked into a container layer, which is exactly what registry-level container image scanning is designed to catch.$q$),
('ci-q-1922','cissp',8,'8.2','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO practices that correctly reflect least-privilege design for a CI/CD service account.$q$,$q$["Scoping the account's credentials to only the specific environment and actions its job actually needs, such as deploying to one designated environment", "Rotating or expiring the account's credentials rather than issuing one permanent, unexpiring token", "Granting the account permanent organization-wide administrator access so it never blocks any future pipeline change", "Sharing one CI/CD credential across every pipeline in the organization to simplify credential management"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0, 1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Least privilege means scoping a service account's access narrowly to what its specific job requires and rotating/expiring its credentials - the opposite of granting broad standing admin access or sharing one credential across every pipeline, both of which maximize the blast radius of a single compromise.$q$),
-- 8.3 software security risk
('ci-q-1923','cissp',8,'8.3','mcq',2,$q$One piece of malware re-encrypts itself with a different key on every infection, keeping the same underlying decrypted payload, while a second rewrites its own code into a functionally equivalent but structurally different form on every infection, with no fixed decryption routine to detect. What are these two techniques called, respectively?$q$,$q$["Polymorphic and metamorphic malware", "Fileless and rootkit-based malware", "Aggregation and inference", "A worm and a trojan"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Polymorphic malware changes its encrypted appearance (a new key each time) while its decrypted payload stays the same. Metamorphic malware goes further, rewriting its own code structure entirely on each infection, making signature-based detection considerably harder for both, but especially the metamorphic variant.$q$),
('ci-q-1924','cissp',8,'8.3','mcq',1,$q$A hidden program silently records every keystroke a user types, including banking passwords, and periodically transmits the captured data to an attacker without ever locking, corrupting, or announcing itself to the user. What type of malware is this?$q$,$q$["Spyware (specifically, a keylogger)", "Ransomware", "A logic bomb", "A rootkit"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Spyware covertly monitors and exfiltrates user activity or data - a keylogger is a common spyware variant that captures keystrokes specifically - distinct from ransomware's disruptive encryption or a logic bomb's dormant trigger-based payload.$q$),
('ci-q-1925','cissp',8,'8.3','mcq',1,$q$A free utility a user installed keeps injecting unwanted advertisements into the browser and covertly reports the user's browsing habits to a marketing network, without granting the attacker persistent remote control or encrypting any files. What type of unwanted software does this best describe?$q$,$q$["Adware", "Ransomware", "A rootkit", "A logic bomb"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Adware's defining trait is injecting unwanted advertising and tracking browsing behavior for marketing purposes - a nuisance and privacy risk, but distinct from malware granting an attacker control (rootkit) or holding data hostage (ransomware).$q$),
('ci-q-1926','cissp',8,'8.3','mcq',2,$q$An organization notices its cloud compute bill has spiked and CPU utilization across many servers is unusually high around the clock, but no data appears to have been stolen or encrypted. Investigation reveals malware secretly using the compute resources to mine cryptocurrency for the attacker. What is this specifically called?$q$,$q$["Cryptojacking", "Ransomware", "A botnet DDoS attack", "A logic bomb"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Cryptojacking is malware or a compromised script that secretly consumes a victim's computing resources to mine cryptocurrency for the attacker - the victim's loss here is resource cost and performance, not stolen or encrypted data.$q$),
('ci-q-1927','cissp',8,'8.3','mcq',2,$q$A multilevel-secure database returns a plausible but fictitious value to a low-clearance user querying a field whose true value is classified, rather than returning an access-denied error that would itself reveal the field is sensitive. What database security technique is this?$q$,$q$["Polyinstantiation", "Aggregation", "A covert channel", "Data remanence"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Polyinstantiation lets a database present different, context-appropriate values for the same logical record to users of different clearance levels - returning a believable cover value instead of an error that would itself leak the fact that sensitive data exists there.$q$),
('ci-q-1928','cissp',8,'8.3','mcq',3,$q$An analyst is denied direct access to any individual employee's salary, but by repeatedly querying an HR system for the average salary of ever-narrower groups - filtering by department, then by department and tenure, then by department, tenure, and job title until only one employee matches - the analyst deduces that individual's exact salary. What does this technique illustrate?$q$,$q$["An inference attack exploiting statistical query narrowing, distinct from directly combining separate data sources", "An aggregation attack, since two low-sensitivity data points were combined", "A buffer overflow", "A dependency confusion attack"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$This is inference: the analyst never directly accessed the restricted salary field, but deduced it by reasoning across a sequence of increasingly narrow, individually permitted statistical queries until only one record could match - a different mechanism from aggregation, which combines multiple separate data sources directly.$q$),
('ci-q-1929','cissp',8,'8.3','mcq',2,$q$One virus variant infects the master boot record of a storage device so it executes before the operating system loads, while a different variant embeds itself in a document's macro code and executes when that document is opened in its host application. What are these two virus categories called, respectively?$q$,$q$["Boot sector virus and macro virus", "Polymorphic virus and metamorphic virus", "Worm and trojan", "Rootkit and bootkit"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A boot sector virus infects the master boot record so it runs before the OS even loads, while a macro virus embeds itself in a document's macro language and triggers when that document is opened in a vulnerable application - two distinct infection vectors for the same broad category (virus).$q$),
('ci-q-1930','cissp',8,'8.3','mcq',2,$q$A trojan installs itself under the guise of a legitimate utility, but specifically opens a persistent backdoor that lets the attacker remotely browse the victim's files, activate the webcam, and execute commands at will, well beyond just running once and exiting. What is this specific, more capable category of trojan called?$q$,$q$["A Remote Access Trojan (RAT)", "A logic bomb", "A worm", "A boot sector virus"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A RAT is a trojan specifically built to establish persistent, interactive remote control over the victim's machine - browsing files, activating peripherals, executing commands - going well beyond a generic trojan that might just run a single malicious payload once.$q$),
('ci-q-1931','cissp',8,'8.3','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO statements that correctly describe different command-and-control (C2) architectures used by botnets.$q$,$q$["A centralized C2 architecture routes commands through one or a few servers, making it easier to disrupt by taking down that infrastructure", "A peer-to-peer C2 architecture has bots relay commands to each other, removing any single point of failure a defender could take down", "Botnets are only ever capable of using a single, fixed IP address for C2 with no alternative techniques", "C2 infrastructure is exclusively used for sending spam email and has no other function"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0, 1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Centralized C2 is easier to disrupt because taking down the few central servers cuts off every bot, while peer-to-peer C2 removes that single point of failure by having bots relay commands to each other. Botnets are not limited to one fixed IP (many rotate infrastructure, including via DGAs), and C2 supports far more than spam - DDoS, data exfiltration, and cryptomining among them.$q$),
('ci-q-1932','cissp',8,'8.3','mcq',2,$q$A botnet's malware calculates a new list of hundreds of pseudo-random domain names every day using a shared algorithm and the current date, then attempts to contact each one until it finds the attacker's currently active C2 server. What technique is being used, and why?$q$,$q$["A Domain Generation Algorithm (DGA), used to evade static domain-blocklisting and takedown of a fixed C2 domain", "Typosquatting, used to trick developers into installing a malicious package", "Dependency confusion, used to hijack a private package name", "A covert channel, used to exfiltrate data via timing"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A DGA generates a large, shifting set of candidate domains algorithmically so the attacker only needs to register a small fraction of them at any time - defeating defenders who try to disrupt the botnet by blocklisting or seizing one fixed, known C2 domain.$q$),
('ci-q-1933','cissp',8,'8.3','matching',2,$q$Match each malware/technique to the description that best fits it.$q$,$q${"left": ["Spyware/keylogger", "Adware", "Cryptojacking", "Rootkit"], "right": ["Modifies OS/kernel-level components to hide its own presence while maintaining privileged access", "Secretly consumes computing resources to mine cryptocurrency for an attacker", "Silently records user input, such as keystrokes, and exfiltrates it without the user's knowledge", "Injects unwanted advertisements and covertly tracks browsing habits"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2, 3, 1, 0]$q$::jsonb,$q$Spyware/keyloggers silently capture and exfiltrate input; adware injects ads and tracks browsing; cryptojacking secretly consumes resources to mine cryptocurrency; a rootkit hides its presence by modifying OS/kernel-level components while retaining privileged access.$q$),
-- 8.4 assess the security impact of acquired software
('ci-q-1934','cissp',8,'8.4','mcq',1,$q$A vendor provides customers with a formal, machine-readable inventory listing every component, library, and version included in a shipped software product, similar to a nutrition label. What is this document called?$q$,$q$["A Software Bill of Materials (SBOM)", "A penetration test report", "A SOC 2 report", "A license compliance scan"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$An SBOM is a formal, machine-readable inventory of every component and version in a piece of software - letting a customer (or an SCA tool) quickly determine whether a newly disclosed vulnerability affects a product they've already deployed.$q$),
('ci-q-1935','cissp',8,'8.4','mcq',3,$q$An SCA tool flags a critical CVE in a transitive dependency, but deeper analysis shows the application's code never actually calls the vulnerable function in that library. What technique determined this, and what is its practical value?$q$,$q$["Reachability analysis - it reduces false-positive remediation urgency by confirming whether vulnerable code is actually invoked by the application", "License compliance scanning - it checks whether the dependency's license is compatible", "Dependency pinning - it locks the dependency to an exact version", "Typosquatting detection - it flags maliciously named packages"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Reachability analysis traces whether an application's actual code paths ever invoke the specific vulnerable function, letting teams distinguish a CVE that's genuinely exploitable in their context from one present in the dependency but never actually reachable - sharpening remediation priority beyond a raw CVE list.$q$),
('ci-q-1936','cissp',8,'8.4','mcq',2,$q$Two permissive open-source licenses both allow free reuse in proprietary products, but one explicitly grants users a patent license from contributors, while the other is silent on patents entirely. Which license includes the explicit patent grant?$q$,$q$["Apache License 2.0", "MIT License", "GNU GPL v3", "LGPL"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Apache License 2.0 includes an explicit patent grant from contributors, giving users additional protection against patent claims related to the licensed code - a feature the shorter, simpler MIT License does not address at all.$q$),
('ci-q-1937','cissp',8,'8.4','mcq',2,$q$A company wants to dynamically link its proprietary application against an open-source library without being obligated to release its own application's source code, while the library itself remains modifiable and shareable under copyleft terms. Which license family is specifically designed to permit this kind of linking?$q$,$q$["LGPL (Lesser GPL)", "GPL", "MIT", "Apache License 2.0"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$LGPL relaxes GPL's copyleft obligation specifically for the case of dynamic linking - a proprietary application can link against an LGPL library without being forced to open its own source, while the LGPL-covered library itself stays copyleft.$q$),
('ci-q-1938','cissp',8,'8.4','mcq',2,$q$An organization applies a lightweight self-attestation questionnaire to a vendor providing free desk-booking software, but requires a full on-site audit and continuous monitoring for a vendor processing customer payment data. What risk management practice does this reflect?$q$,$q$["Vendor risk tiering, scaling assessment depth to the criticality of the data or access a vendor is granted", "A single, uniform vendor assessment applied identically regardless of scope", "License compliance scanning", "Dependency pinning"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Vendor risk tiering matches the depth and rigor of a security assessment to how much risk a given vendor relationship actually carries - a low-risk, low-access vendor gets a lighter review, while a vendor touching sensitive data gets the most rigorous scrutiny.$q$),
('ci-q-1939','cissp',8,'8.4','mcq',2,$q$A company depends on a small, financially uncertain vendor for a critical proprietary application and negotiates a contractual arrangement where a neutral third party holds a copy of the vendor's source code, to be released to the customer only if the vendor goes out of business or stops supporting the product. What is this arrangement called?$q$,$q$["Software (source code) escrow", "A right-to-audit clause", "A service level agreement", "A software bill of materials"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A software escrow agreement places a copy of source code with a neutral third party, releasable to the customer under defined trigger conditions (like vendor bankruptcy) - protecting continuity of a critical dependency even if the original vendor disappears.$q$),
('ci-q-1940','cissp',8,'8.4','mcq',1,$q$A vendor contract includes a clause granting the customer the ability to inspect the vendor's security controls and practices directly, or through an independent auditor, at agreed intervals. What is this contractual provision called?$q$,$q$["A right-to-audit clause", "A software escrow agreement", "A dependency pinning policy", "A license compliance scan"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A right-to-audit clause contractually preserves the customer's ability to verify - directly or via an independent auditor - that the vendor's actual security practices match what was represented, rather than relying purely on the vendor's self-reported assurances.$q$),
('ci-q-1941','cissp',8,'8.4','mcq',2,$q$An organization standardizes on producing its SBOMs in one of two widely adopted, tool-interoperable machine-readable formats so that customers' automated systems can parse them consistently. Which two formats are these?$q$,$q$["SPDX and CycloneDX", "GPL and LGPL", "SAST and DAST", "SAML and OAuth"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$SPDX and CycloneDX are the two widely adopted, standardized machine-readable SBOM formats, chosen specifically so automated tooling on the consuming end can parse and act on the component inventory consistently, regardless of which format the producing vendor used.$q$),
('ci-q-1942','cissp',8,'8.4','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO items that belong in a thorough security evaluation of a COTS (commercial off-the-shelf) product before acquisition.$q$,$q$["The vendor's patch and vulnerability disclosure history", "Evidence of independent security assessment, such as a recent penetration test or SOC 2 report", "The personal political opinions of the vendor's individual engineers", "The font used in the vendor's marketing materials"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0, 1]$q$::jsonb,$q$A COTS vendor evaluation should weigh their patch/disclosure track record and independent assurance evidence like a SOC 2 report or recent pen test - concrete signals of security posture - not irrelevant factors like marketing aesthetics or employees' personal opinions.$q$),
('ci-q-1943','cissp',8,'8.4','mcq',1,$q$Before adopting an open-source library, a team checks how recently it was last updated, how many active maintainers and contributors it has, and how quickly reported vulnerabilities have historically been patched. What is this evaluation assessing?$q$,$q$["The open-source project's ongoing health and maintenance activity, as a proxy for how reliably future vulnerabilities will get fixed", "The library's exact license text, word for word", "The library's binary file size", "The programming language the library is written in"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Project health signals - recent activity, active maintainer count, and historical patch responsiveness - are a practical proxy for how reliably a project will keep getting security fixes going forward, distinct from license terms or purely technical attributes like size or language.$q$),
('ci-q-1944','cissp',8,'8.4','mcq',2,$q$A dependency was added years ago for a feature that has since been removed from the application, but the import was never deleted, so the library and its own transitive dependencies are still bundled and shipped. What risk does this create?$q$,$q$["It expands the application's attack surface with code that provides no functional benefit, since any vulnerability in that unused dependency is still present in the shipped application", "It has no security relevance since the feature that used it is gone", "It automatically improves application performance", "It removes the need for any future SCA scanning"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$An unused dependency still ships with the application and still executes as part of it - a vulnerability discovered in it remains fully present and exploitable, even though it provides zero functional value anymore. Dead code is attack surface without offsetting benefit.$q$),
('ci-q-1945','cissp',8,'8.4','mcq',2,$q$A team ran an SCA scan once, at the initial release of an application, and has not rescanned since, even though eighteen months and several newly disclosed CVEs in its dependencies have passed. What gap does this illustrate?$q$,$q$["A point-in-time scan can't catch vulnerabilities disclosed after the scan date - dependencies need continuous or scheduled re-scanning, not a single one-time check", "SCA tools are only capable of running once per application, ever", "New CVEs disclosed after release pose no risk to already-deployed software", "Dependency pinning eliminates the need for any SCA scanning at all"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A dependency that was clean at release can become vulnerable the moment a new CVE is disclosed against it - an SCA scan only reflects what was known at the time it ran, so ongoing exposure requires continuous or regularly scheduled re-scanning, not a single check frozen at release.$q$),
('ci-q-1946','cissp',8,'8.4','ordering',2,$q$Order the following steps of a typical third-party software vetting and procurement process from first to last.$q$,$q$["Identify the business need and candidate vendors", "Conduct the vendor security assessment (questionnaire, SOC 2/audit review)", "Negotiate contractual security terms (SLAs, right-to-audit, breach notification)", "Onboard the vendor and integrate the software", "Periodically reassess the vendor throughout the relationship"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0, 1, 2, 3, 4]$q$::jsonb,$q$Vetting starts with identifying the need and candidates, then assessing their security posture, then locking in contractual security terms before signing, then onboarding/integrating the product, and finally reassessing the vendor periodically for as long as the relationship continues - risk doesn't end at signature.$q$),
('ci-q-1947','cissp',8,'8.4','matching',2,$q$Match each open-source license to the characteristic that most specifically distinguishes it.$q$,$q${"left": ["MIT License", "Apache License 2.0", "GNU GPL", "LGPL"], "right": ["Copyleft for the library itself, but permits dynamic linking from proprietary code without that obligation", "Highly permissive with minimal restrictions and no explicit patent grant", "Copyleft, requiring derivative/combined works to be released under the same license", "Permissive, but includes an explicit patent grant from contributors"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[1, 3, 2, 0]$q$::jsonb,$q$MIT is minimally restrictive with no patent language; Apache 2.0 is permissive but adds an explicit patent grant; GPL is copyleft, requiring derivative/combined works to also be GPL-licensed; LGPL keeps the library itself copyleft but specifically permits proprietary dynamic linking without that obligation.$q$),
-- 8.5 secure coding guidelines and standards
('ci-q-1948','cissp',8,'8.5','mcq',2,$q$A web application passes user-supplied input directly into a system shell call to ping a hostname, and an attacker submits input containing a semicolon followed by an additional shell command, which then executes as if the developer had typed it. What type of injection is this?$q$,$q$["OS command injection", "SQL injection", "LDAP injection", "Cross-site scripting"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$OS command injection occurs when unsanitized user input is passed into a system shell call, letting an attacker append and execute arbitrary operating-system commands using metacharacters like a semicolon - distinct from SQL, LDAP, or script-based injection, which target different interpreters.$q$),
('ci-q-1949','cissp',8,'8.5','mcq',2,$q$An application queries a MongoDB collection using values taken directly from user input, and an attacker submits a crafted JSON operator (such as one meaning "always true") instead of a normal login value, bypassing authentication entirely. What type of vulnerability does this exploit?$q$,$q$["NoSQL injection", "Cross-site request forgery", "Insecure deserialization", "Server-side request forgery"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$NoSQL injection targets document/NoSQL databases by submitting crafted query operators instead of plain values - here, an operator that evaluates to always-true - bypassing logic like authentication just as classic SQL injection does against relational databases.$q$),
('ci-q-1950','cissp',8,'8.5','mcq',2,$q$An application builds a directory-service search filter by directly concatenating user-supplied username input, and an attacker submits input containing LDAP filter metacharacters that alter the search logic, causing the query to return every user record instead of just one. What type of vulnerability is this?$q$,$q$["LDAP injection", "SQL injection", "IDOR", "Broken access control"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$LDAP injection exploits unsanitized input concatenated into a directory-service search filter, using LDAP-specific metacharacters to alter the query's logic - here, widening a single-record lookup into a full directory dump, analogous to SQL injection but against a different query language.$q$),
('ci-q-1951','cissp',8,'8.5','mcq',3,$q$A team fixes an insecure deserialization vulnerability not by attempting to block a list of known-dangerous class names, but by explicitly restricting deserialization to a small, pre-approved set of expected classes and rejecting anything else. Why is this allow-list approach considered more effective than a deny-list?$q$,$q$["A deny-list can only ever block known-dangerous classes and is bypassed by any dangerous class not yet on the list, while an allow-list rejects everything by default except what's explicitly trusted", "An allow-list is easier to bypass than a deny-list", "Deny-lists require no ongoing maintenance, unlike allow-lists", "Allow-lists and deny-lists provide identical security guarantees"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A deny-list is inherently reactive - it can only stop classes someone already identified as dangerous, leaving any new or overlooked dangerous class free to pass through. An allow-list flips the default to deny, so only explicitly reviewed and trusted classes can ever be deserialized, closing that gap.$q$),
('ci-q-1952','cissp',8,'8.5','mcq',2,$q$An application transmits session tokens and password reset links over unencrypted HTTP and stores credit card numbers in the database without encryption at rest. Which OWASP Top 10 (2021) category does this best fall under?$q$,$q$["Cryptographic Failures", "Broken Access Control", "Insecure Design", "Security Misconfiguration"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Cryptographic Failures covers exactly this: sensitive data transmitted or stored without adequate encryption, exposing it to interception or theft - distinct from access-control gaps, architectural design flaws, or unsafe default configuration.$q$),
('ci-q-1953','cissp',8,'8.5','mcq',2,$q$An application continues running a web framework version with multiple publicly disclosed, unpatched critical vulnerabilities, despite newer patched versions being available for over a year. Which OWASP Top 10 (2021) category does this specifically represent?$q$,$q$["Vulnerable and Outdated Components", "Identification and Authentication Failures", "Software and Data Integrity Failures", "Server-Side Request Forgery"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Vulnerable and Outdated Components covers exactly this scenario - running known-vulnerable software or libraries well past the point a patched version was available - a direct, avoidable risk distinct from authentication weaknesses or integrity-verification failures.$q$),
('ci-q-1954','cissp',8,'8.5','mcq',2,$q$An application allows unlimited login attempts with no lockout or delay, and permits weak passwords like 'password1' with no complexity or breach-list check. Which OWASP Top 10 (2021) category do these weaknesses fall under?$q$,$q$["Identification and Authentication Failures", "Broken Access Control", "Injection", "Insecure Design"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Identification and Authentication Failures covers weaknesses in confirming who a user is and verifying they are who they claim - unlimited login attempts (no brute-force protection) and permitting trivially weak passwords are textbook examples of this category, distinct from access-control or injection issues.$q$),
('ci-q-1955','cissp',8,'8.5','mcq',2,$q$An e-commerce checkout flow has no fundamental limit built into its design on how many times a discount code can be redeemed per account, a gap that exists regardless of how carefully the code implementing it is written or tested. Which OWASP Top 10 (2021) category, added to highlight flaws in the architecture stage rather than in implementation, best fits this?$q$,$q$["Insecure Design", "Security Misconfiguration", "Injection", "Vulnerable and Outdated Components"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Insecure Design covers architectural and requirements-level flaws - like never having designed a redemption limit at all - that no amount of careful implementation or bug-fixing can remedy, since the missing control was never part of the design in the first place.$q$),
('ci-q-1956','cissp',8,'8.5','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO items below that are genuine OWASP Top 10 (2021) category names.$q$,$q$["Broken Access Control", "Cryptographic Failures", "Insufficient Firewall Rules", "Weak Password Policy Violation"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0, 1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Broken Access Control and Cryptographic Failures are both official OWASP Top 10 (2021) categories. "Insufficient Firewall Rules" and "Weak Password Policy Violation" are not actual category names in the list, even though they sound plausible.$q$),
('ci-q-1957','cissp',8,'8.5','mcq',2,$q$A mobile app's backend API returns a full user object - including an internal 'isAdmin' field never shown in the UI - and separately accepts any field submitted in a profile-update request, including that same 'isAdmin' field, applying whatever value the client sends. What API-specific vulnerability class does the second half of this scenario (accepting and applying unexpected client-submitted fields) represent?$q$,$q$["Mass assignment", "Broken Object Level Authorization", "Rate limiting bypass", "SSRF"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Mass assignment occurs when an API automatically binds every field in a client-submitted request to an internal object's properties, without restricting which fields are actually allowed to be set - letting an attacker set a sensitive field like 'isAdmin' simply by including it in the request body.$q$),
('ci-q-1958','cissp',8,'8.5','mcq',2,$q$A mobile banking API endpoint retrieves account details using an account ID supplied in the request URL, but never verifies that the authenticated caller is actually authorized to view that specific account ID, letting one user retrieve another user's account details by changing the ID. What is this API-specific vulnerability called in the OWASP API Security Top 10?$q$,$q$["Broken Object Level Authorization (BOLA)", "Mass assignment", "Excessive rate limiting", "Insecure deserialization"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$BOLA is the OWASP API Security Top 10's term for exactly this pattern - an API endpoint that authenticates the caller but fails to verify they're authorized to access the specific object referenced by an ID in the request, letting object-level access controls be bypassed by simply changing the ID.$q$),
('ci-q-1959','cissp',8,'8.5','mcq',2,$q$A rate limiter resets its request counter to zero at the start of every new calendar minute, which lets a client send its full quota right at the end of one minute and its full quota again immediately at the start of the next, doubling its effective burst. A different algorithm instead tracks requests within a continuously moving time window ending at the current moment, avoiding this doubling effect. What are these two algorithms called, respectively?$q$,$q$["Fixed window and sliding window", "Token bucket and leaky bucket", "SAST and DAST", "Allow-list and deny-list"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A fixed window resets its counter at fixed boundaries (like the top of every minute), which allows a burst that straddles the boundary to effectively double a client's request rate for a brief moment. A sliding window avoids this by continuously tracking requests within a moving window ending at the current instant, smoothing out that edge-boundary loophole.$q$),
('ci-q-1960','cissp',8,'8.5','matching',3,$q$Match each OWASP Top 10 (2021) category to the scenario that best illustrates it.$q$,$q${"left": ["Insecure Design", "Cryptographic Failures", "Identification and Authentication Failures", "Vulnerable and Outdated Components"], "right": ["A framework version with known, unpatched critical vulnerabilities remains in production for over a year", "A login form permits unlimited attempts with no lockout and accepts trivially weak passwords", "A checkout flow has no architectural limit on discount-code reuse, regardless of implementation quality", "Sensitive data such as credit card numbers is stored and transmitted without encryption"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2, 3, 1, 0]$q$::jsonb,$q$Insecure Design is illustrated by a missing architectural control (no redemption limit by design); Cryptographic Failures by unencrypted sensitive data; Identification and Authentication Failures by weak login protections; Vulnerable and Outdated Components by running known-unpatched software well past when a fix was available.$q$);
